.TH LINSCP 1 "2026-10-16" "LinSCP 1.0" "LinSCP"
.SH NAME
linscp \- secure file transfer client for the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B linscp
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ sftp:// [ user [: password ]@] host [: port ]/ path ]
.br
.B linscp
.B \-batch
.BI \-script= file
.br
.B linscp
.B \-batch
.BI \-command= commands
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B linscp
is the console file transfer client of LinSCP. It connects to an SFTP or an
SCP server over SSH, and transfers files to and from the local file system,
either from a script, from a command line, or interactively.
.PP
The SSH, SFTP and SCP protocol code is the PuTTY core plus this client's own
SCP engine, driven by a POSIX front end. Neither SSH protocol is handed to
another program.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-script= file
Read the commands to run from
.IR file ,
one command per line. Use
.B \-
to read from standard input.
.TP
.BI \-command= commands
Run
.IR commands .
Several commands may be given, separated by
.BR ; .
.TP
.BI \-hostkey= fingerprint
Accept only the host key with this fingerprint, which may be given as the
whole
.RI ' type\ bits\ SHA256: ...'
string, as
.RI ' SHA256: ...',
or as the bare fingerprint. Use
.B *
to accept any key for this connection only; it is not added to the persistent
host-key cache. Without this option an unknown host key is accepted only
after the client has been able to ask; in
.B \-batch
mode the connection fails instead. Either way the key the server offered is
reported on a
.B HOSTKEY
line, so a script or the graphical interface can pin it afterwards instead of
accepting any key next time. An MD5 fingerprint may be given with or without
the
.B MD5:
marker
.B ssh-keygen
prints, and a SHA-256 one as
.RI ' SHA256: base64',
as the hex digest, with or without
.B :
separators. Both
.B sftp://
and
.B scp://
sessions are checked by this client's own SSH code, so what a pin enforces
does not depend on any external program's build.
.TP
.BI \-privatekey= file
Private key to authenticate with. Both PuTTY
.B .ppk
files and OpenSSH/ssh.com keys (including the OpenSSH new format used by
.IR id_rsa " and " id_ed25519 )
are accepted; a key in a foreign format is imported for the connection. An
encrypted key asks for its passphrase interactively and cannot be used in
batch mode: load such a key into an ssh-agent instead.
.TP
.BI \-username= name
User name to authenticate with.
.TP
.BI \-password= password
Password to authenticate with. The
.B LINSCP_PASSWORD
environment variable is used when this option is not given, which keeps the
password out of the command line.
.TP
.BI \-port= number
Port to connect to (1 through 65535). The default is 22.
.TP
.BI \-timeout= seconds
Positive connection timeout in seconds. The default is 15 seconds.
.TP
.BI \-log= file
Write the session log to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-xmllog= file
Write an XML action log of the run to
.IR file :
a
.B log_session
document holding one
.BR command ", " result ", " message ", " session " and " transfer
element for every line executed, its outcome, everything printed, the site
connected to and each file transferred, in the order they happened. A password
appearing in a site URL or in a
.BI \-password=
switch is masked before it is written, also under
.BR "option echo on" .
.TP
.BI \-parameter " value ..."
Script parameters of a
.BI \-script=
or
.BI \-command=
run, referred to from the commands as
.BI %1%
through
.BI %9% .
A bare
.BI \-parameter
takes every remaining argument;
.BI \-parameter= value
adds one. Parameters, the
.BI %TIMESTAMP%
placeholder and
.BI %NAME%
environment variables are expanded on script and interactive lines before the
command is dispatched, in single quotes they are not, and input read by
.BI \-server
is not expanded at all.
.TP
.B \-literal
With
.BI \-command=
only, treat placeholders as literal text rather than expanding script
parameters, timestamps and environment variables. The GUI uses this for
filenames containing percent signs; script files can quote the placeholders
with single quotes instead. Requires
.BI \-command= .
.TP
.B \-batch
Never ask a question. Operations that require an answer fail instead. To
disable overwrite and delete confirmations in a trusted automation script,
run
.B option confirm off
before those operations.
.TP
.B \-gui
Launch the graphical Commander desktop interface. It is a dual\-pane browser
served on a local port: the remote pane, the transfer queue, the built\-in
editor, synchronization and the site manager all drive the commands described
here, and each tab keeps one connection open. The server listens on
.B 127.0.0.1
only and every
.I /api/
call has to carry the token of this run in an
.B X\-LSCP\-Token
header; the token is generated at start\-up, injected into the page the
server serves, and shown by
.BR \-print\-token .
Pass
.I \-host=
to listen on another address and
.I \-allow\-host=NAME
to answer requests that a reverse proxy addresses to another name, or
.I \-allow\-any\-host
to skip that check. A new site starts without a host key, so its first
connection reports the fingerprint it was offered and waits for it to be
pinned.
.TP
.B \-server
Read commands from standard input, one per line, and answer each of them with
a single
.B LSCP\-RESULT
line carrying the exit code. The session stays open between commands, which is
how the graphical interface browses and edits without reconnecting. The first
line printed is
.BR "LSCP\-SERVER 1 ready" .
This switch cannot be combined with
.BR \-command= .
.TP
.B \-verbose
Print what the SSH connection layer does as it happens.
.TP
.B \-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-help
Print the usage and exit.
.SH SITES
A site is a URL of the form
.PP
.RS
.BI sftp:// [ user [: password ]@] host [: port ]/ path
.RE
.PP
The schemes
.BR sftp:// ,
.BR ssh:// ,
.BR scp:// ,
.BR ftp:// ,
.BR ftps:// ,
.BR dav:// ,
.B webdav://
and
.B s3://
are all accepted, as is a bare
.IR host [ :port ].
The path is the directory the client changes to after connecting; a URL with
no path starts in the home directory the login is given. The
password is optional in the URL; percent-encode reserved characters in URL
credentials and paths. Prefer
.B \-password=
or
.B LINSCP_PASSWORD
so that the password is not written into a script file.
.PP
An
.B scp://
.RI (or\  ssh:// )
session is served by this client itself. SCP is not a filesystem protocol but
a login shell plus the rcp transfer protocol that runs on it, so a listing is
an
.B ls \-l \-a
the client parses, a
.B stat
is an
.BR "ls \-l \-d" ,
a
.B cd
is a
.B cd
the server's own shell answers for, and the directory and metadata commands
are the shell's
.BR mkdir ,
.BR rmdir ,
.BR rm ,
.BR mv ,
.BR chmod ,
.BR "cp \-p" ,
.B ln \-s
and
.BR touch .
The bytes of a transfer go through the server's own
.B scp
on an SSH channel whose protocol the client speaks. Each of those commands is
one SSH connection of its own, and every path sent is quoted for a POSIX
shell; a path holding a control character is refused instead of being sent.
The rcp protocol has no offset, so a transfer cannot be continued:
.B \-resume
is refused by name, and a
.B .filepart
file an interrupted run left behind is discarded and the transfer restarted
rather than spliced onto bytes nobody can position.
.SS Stored sessions
A site can be given a name and stored, so that
.B open
can be followed by a name instead of a URL. Sessions are kept in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/linscp/sessions.ini
(or
.IR $HOME/.config/linscp/sessions.ini ),
which is the same file the graphical interface reads and writes. Create one
with
.BR savesession ,
list them with
.BR session ,
and remove one with
.BR rmsession .
A password is only stored when
.I \-password=
is given to
.BR savesession .
.SH COMMANDS
A command name, an
.B option
name and the values of the options that have a fixed vocabulary may be
abbreviated to any prefix that identifies one of them:
.B synch
is
.BR synchronize ,
.B "option bat cont"
is
.BR "option batch continue" .
An abbreviation of
.B option
or of a command that fits several names is refused and answered with the list
of what it could mean, because guessing would run a different command than the
one that was typed.
.TP
.BI open " site [ switches ]"
Connect to
.IR site ,
which is either a URL or the name of a stored session (see
.B STORED SESSIONS
above). The switches are
.BR \-hostkey= ,
.BR \-password= ,
.BR \-username= ,
.BR \-privatekey= " and"
.BR \-rawsettings ;
they apply to a stored session exactly as they apply to a URL, so
.I open webserver \-password=secret
authenticates the stored site with that password without storing it.
.IP
.B \-rawsettings
takes
.I name=value
session settings under the names upstream uses, either to the end of the
command or, with
.BR \-rawsettings= N,
exactly
.I N
of them. This build applies the proxy settings
.BR ProxyMethod= None|SOCKS4|SOCKS5|HTTP|Telnet|Cmd,
.BR ProxyHost= ,
.BR ProxyPort= ,
.BR ProxyUsername= ,
.BR ProxyPassword= ,
.BR ProxyTelnetCommand= ,
.BR ProxyLocalCommand= ,
.BR ProxyDNS= 0|1|2
(off, automatic, on),
.B ProxyLocalhost=
0|1 and
.BR ProxyExcludeList= ,
and refuses any other setting by name rather than accepting one it would
drop. The native SFTP and SCP transports negotiate every method; a protocol
served through curl uses SOCKS4, SOCKS5 and HTTP proxies and refuses a telnet
or local-command proxy instead of connecting around it.
.I ProxyLocalhost=1
is needed to send a connection to localhost through a proxy, as upstream
does not proxy it otherwise.
.TP
.B close
Disconnect the open session, or the session at the 1-based
.I index
given as an argument (one connection is open at a time).
.TP
.BI cd " directory"
Change the remote directory.
.TP
.B pwd
Print the remote directory.
.TP
.BI ls " [" \-al1 "] [" directory ]
List the remote directory.
.B \-l
is the long format, which is the default;
.B \-1
prints names only;
.B \-a
is accepted and does nothing, as entries starting with a dot are always
listed.
.TP
.BI lls " [directory[/mask]]"
List local files in the columns upstream prints: date, time, size (or
.BR <DIR> )
and name, optionally filtered by a simple wildcard mask. A mask that
matches nothing prints a sentence instead of failing, unless
.B option failonnomatch on
turns that sentence into the script error.
.TP
.BI lcd " directory"
Change the local directory and print it.
.TP
.B lpwd
Print the local directory.
.TP
.BI get " [" options "] remote [" local ]
Download a file, a directory recursively, or every file matching a mask.
Optional switches include
.BR \-r " (recursive directory download),"
.BR \-resume " (resume interrupted transfers at target offset),"
.BR \-delete " (remove remote source after transfer),"
.BR \-neweronly " (skip files that are not newer than the target),"
.BR \-noneweronly " (copy regardless of their dates, the default),"
.BR \-append " (add to the end of the target instead of replacing it),"
.BR \-speed= KB " (limit the transfer to that many kilobytes per second, 0 for"
no limit),
.BR \-resumesupport= on|off|KB "( (whether, and from what size, an"
interrupted file is continued instead of restarted),
and
.BR \-preservetime " / " \-preservetime=all " (keep the timestamps;"
with
.I =all
those of directories too),
.BR \-permissions " / " \-permissions= mode " / " \-nopermissions ,
and
.BR \-rawtransfersettings[=N] .
On a download,
.B \-permissions
without a value gives the local file the permissions of the remote one
(when the server reports them),
.I =mode
sets the octal mode outright
.RB ( 600 ", " 755 )
and
.B \-nopermissions
leaves whatever the file got while being created.
.B \-rawtransfersettings
accepts upstream's own
.I key=value
parameter list
.RB ( TransferMode ", " PreserveTime ", " ResumeSupport ", " CPSLimit
and the rest, optionally capped with
.IR =N );
a setting this build does not apply is refused by name instead of being
silently dropped.
.B \-append
and
.B \-resume
cannot be combined, and both need a transport that can position a partial
file: the native SFTP one. Over
.B scp://
they are refused with that statement rather than silently turning into an
overwrite or into bytes appended where nobody asked for them.
When omitted,
.I local
defaults to the current local directory.
.TP
.BI put " [" options "] local [" remote ]
Upload a file, a directory recursively, or every file matching a mask.
Optional switches include
.BR \-r " (recursive directory upload),"
.BR \-resume " (resume interrupted transfers at target offset),"
.BR \-delete " (remove local source after transfer),"
.BR \-neweronly " and " \-noneweronly ","
.BR \-append " (add to the end of the remote file),"
.BR \-speed= KB ","
.BR \-resumesupport= on|off|KB ","
.BR \-preservetime " / " \-preservetime=all ","
.BR \-permissions " / " \-permissions= mode " / " \-nopermissions ,"
and
.BR \-rawtransfersettings[=N] .
On an upload,
.B \-permissions
without a value puts the local file's own permissions on the server,
.I =mode
sets the octal mode and
.B \-nopermissions
asks the server for its defaults; the raw settings are the ones listed
under
.BR get .
The
.BR \-append " and " \-resume
switches need a transport that can position a partial file, which over
.B scp://
nothing can, and they exclude each other.
When omitted,
.I remote
defaults to the current remote directory.
.TP
.BI synchronize " " local|remote|both " [" options "] [" localdir " [" remotedir ]]"
Synchronize directories in the specified direction.
.B remote
pushes changed local files to the server,
.B local
pulls changed remote files to the local machine, and
.B both
synchronizes both directions by comparing with the record of the previous
synchronization. Both directories default to the current local and remote
directories. The following switches are supported:
.RS
.TP
.BI \-criteria= criteria
How to decide whether a file changed. The default is
.BR time ,
which copies a file the source has newer (in
.B \-mirror
mode any difference in the timestamps counts). Three words name a whole set
of criteria on their own:
.B time
compares the modification times,
.B none
copies every file, and
.B either
\- whose synonym is
.B both
\- compares the modification time and the size. The three comparisons
.BR time ,
.B size
and
.B checksum
may also be listed, separated by commas, as in
.IR time,checksum ;
whatever the list names is compared and nothing else.
.B checksum
reads the contents of the file on both sides, so it sees a change that left
the timestamp and the size alone; the server computes the digest when it has
a checksum extension and a downloaded copy is hashed otherwise, which the
client says once per synchronization. A file that cannot be hashed stops the
synchronization with the reason instead of counting as equal to the other
side. In the
.B both
direction the record of the previous synchronization decides, and it records
timestamps and sizes, so
.B checksum
and
.B none
are refused there rather than quietly ignored.
.TP
.BI \-filemask= masks
Only synchronize files matching one of the masks. Separate several masks with
a semicolon and exclude with a leading
.BR | ,
for example
.IR "*" .txt "|" "*.bak" .
A mask may not contain a path separator.
.TP
.B \-delete
Delete files on the target side that were removed on the source side. In
.B both
mode a file is only deleted when the record of the previous synchronization
proves the other side removed it, so a file that was never synchronized is
left alone, and nothing is deleted while a conflict is unresolved.
.TP
.B \-recursive
Include subdirectories. Without it the two directories are compared at the
top level only, and a directory that exists on one side is skipped.
.TP
.B \-preview
Print the plan and change nothing. One
.B SYNCPLAN
line describes the directories, the mode, the criteria and the mask, then one
line per item with the action, the path and the reason, and a final
.B SYNCSUMMARY
line with the number of uploads, downloads, deletions, conflicts and bytes.
.RE
.TP
.BI keepuptodate " [" options "] [" localdir " [" remotedir ]]"
Reflect changes in the local directory on the remote directory, as upstream
WinSCP does. The first synchronization pass runs immediately. A recursive
inotify watcher starts another pass when a local change is reported. The
.BI \-interval= seconds
(10 by default, from 1 to 86400) also triggers a full comparison as a safety
rescan; timer polling is used when the watch cannot be set up. Give
.BI \-count= n
to stop after
.I n
passes. An interactive run continues until Ctrl-C; a non-interactive run must
give
.BR \-count= .
.TP
.BI edit " [file]"
Download
.I file
and open it in a text editor, then upload it again when the editor exits. The
editor is taken from the
.B LINSCP_EDITOR
or
.B EDITOR
variable, from
.IR option\\ editor ,
or from the first common editor that is installed. Files larger than
.I option\\ editmaxsize
(100 MiB by default) are refused. The file is left untouched when the editor
does not change it, and an upload is refused when the remote file was modified
after the download began. Without an argument the current remote directory is
used.
.TP
.BI lock " file [\-timeout=" seconds "]"
Take an exclusive write lock on one WebDAV resource and remember its token.
Uploads, deletes and moves of that path then carry the token (the
.B If
header), so the server refuses a write the lock was meant to prevent instead
of accepting it. A server that does not implement
.B LOCK
answers 405 and the command says so. Upstream has no such command, so this is
a LinSCP extension.
.TP
.BI unlock " file"
Release the lock
.B lock
took in this session. A lock taken by another client cannot be released, and
this session cannot release a lock it did not take.
.TP
.BI etag " file \-match=" etag
Record an
.B If-Match
precondition for one WebDAV path with this session. Uploads, deletes and moves
of that path then carry the tag in the
.B If
header, in the same condition list as a lock token, so the server refuses (412)
a write over a version this client has not read instead of accepting it. The
value is the tag
.B etag
.I file
.B \-get
prints: the command splitter takes the quotes off an argument, so this client
puts them back and keeps a
.B W/
weakness prefix outside them.
.TP
.BI etag " file \-nomatch=" etag
Record an
.B If-None-Match
precondition: the write goes through only where the server's tag differs, which
is how a script writes over a resource nobody has written since it read it.
.B *
is refused rather than sent, because the
.B If
header has no way to say \(dqonly where there is nothing yet\(dq and a quoted
asterisk is a tag no resource carries: the condition would hold on every write
and guard nothing while looking like a guard.
.TP
.BI etag " file \-get"
Print the ETag the server reports for
.I file
now, read with a Depth-0
.BR PROPFIND .
A path that is not there says so, and a server that reports no tag for a path
that is says that instead; the two are different answers.
.TP
.BI etag " file \-clear"
Forget the precondition recorded for
.IR file .
Give exactly one of the four actions per call. A precondition guards the path
it was recorded for and nothing below it: a collection's tag names its listing,
so the first member written under an inherited one would stale it and fail
every later write in that directory. A session whose protocol has no entity
tags (the SSH engines, FTP and S3) is refused by name rather than left
believing a write is conditional. Upstream has no ETag preconditions, so
.B etag
is a LinSCP extension.
.TP
.BI property " file \-get=" name
Read one WebDAV dead property of
.IR file .
The property lives in this client's own namespace
.RI ( urn:linscp:properties ),
so a protected
.B DAV:
property is never the target. A property the server does not have prints
.RI \(dq name " is not set" .
.TP
.BI property " file \-set=" name = value
Store one dead property on
.IR file .
The value is XML-escaped; the name has to start with a letter or
.B _
and may hold letters, digits,
.BR _ ,
.B \-
and
.BR . .
A server that does not implement
.B PROPPATCH
answers 405 and the command says so. Both forms of
.B property
are LinSCP extensions.
.TP
.B session
List the open sessions as index and name and print which one is active.
With a 1-based
.I index
as the only connection that is open, it prints the active session.
.TP
.B sessions
List the stored sessions (also
.BR "sessions export" ,
.BR "sessions import" ).
.TP
.BI savesession " name [" options ]"
Store the current session, or the session described by the options, under
.IR name .
The options are
.BR \-protocol= ,
.BR \-host= ,
.BR \-port= ,
.BR \-username= ,
.BR \-password= ,
.BR \-privatekey= ,
.BR \-hostkey= ,
.BR \-remotedir= ,
.BR \-localdir= ,
.BR \-preset= " and"
.BR \-rawsettings .
.I \-preset=
stores the transfer preset the site loads when it is opened (an empty
value clears a stored one), so a site keeps its copy settings like the
rest of its session data.
.I \-rawsettings
stores session settings with the site \- the proxy settings described
under
.B open
above \- and a proxy password is encrypted under the same key file as a
session password. A re-save that gives neither keeps the proxy the site
was stored with.
The password is stored only when
.I \-password=
is given, and a host key is stored only when
.I \-hostkey=
is given, so a session saved after connecting with
.B \\-hostkey=*
stays unpinned. Unknown switches are rejected.
.TP
.BI rmsession " name"
Remove the stored session called
.IR name .
.TP
.BI savepreset " name setting" ...
Store or update a transfer preset under
.IR name .
A setting is either
.I key=value
or a bare switch; the keys are
.BR transfer= ,
.BR preservetime= ,
.BR neweronly ,
.BR speed= ,
.BR resumesupport= ,
.BR permissions= ,
.BR host= ,
.BR user= ,
.BR remotedir= ,
.BR localdir=
and
.BR mask= .
Saving over an existing preset changes only the settings the command
names. Unknown settings and invalid values are refused with the key at
fault.
.TP
.BI rmspreset " name"
Remove the transfer preset called
.IR name .
When it was the active preset, transfers go back to their own settings.
.TP
.B presets
List the transfer presets of the user configuration with their settings.
Presets live in
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/linscp/transfer-presets.ini
and apply to get/put and their aliases: by name through
.BR "option preset" ,
by name with the site through
.BR "savesession \-preset=" ,
or by their host, user, directory and mask rules when no preset was
picked by name. The switches of a transfer always win over the preset.
.TP
.BI echo " [" message ]
Print a message to the console output.
.TP
.BI rm " file"
Remove remote files.
.TP
.BI rmdir " directory"
Remove a remote directory.
.TP
.BI mkdir " [\-parents] directory"
Create a remote directory. With
.B \-parents
create missing ancestors too, leaving existing directories intact; without
it the parent must already exist.
.TP
.BI mv " from to"
Move or rename a remote file.
.TP
.BI chmod " mode file [" file ...] [ \-recursive ]
Change the permissions of remote files. The mode is octal
.RB ( 644 ", " 0700 ", " 1777 )
or symbolic
.RB ( u+x ", " go\-w ", " a=r,go\-w " or " u+s ),
where
.I X
means the execute bit only where some execute bit is already set or for a
directory. With
.B \-recursive
everything below a directory is changed as well and symbolic links are left
alone, so that a recursive run cannot leave the tree. The mode is checked
before the connection is used, so a typo is reported even when offline.
.TP
.BI copyid " [keyfile]"
Install the public half of the given key file, or of the private key the
session authenticates with when no file is given, into
the server's
.B ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
of the connected user.
.B ~/.ssh
is created with mode 0700 and the file with 0600, and a key that is already
there is not added a second time. OpenSSH public and private key files and PuTTY
.B .ppk
files are read, an encrypted key is asked for its passphrase interactively and
refused in batch mode, and an
.B sftp://
or
.B scp://
session is required. Key files are read through the bundled PuTTY core.
.TP
.BI stat " file"
Print the listing line of a remote path, the line
.B ls \-l
would show for it.
.TP
.BI attr " file"
Print the attributes of a remote file: path, size, permissions, modification
time and owner. The
.B SizeBytes
and
.B ModifiedEpoch
lines repeat the size and the time in a form a script can compare.
.TP
.B terminal
Open an interactive shell on the connected server in a terminal window,
using the system
.B ssh
client. The terminal program is taken from the
.B LINSCP_TERMINAL
variable (for example
.BR "xterm -e"
or
.BR foot ),
otherwise the first common desktop terminal that is installed. SSH
.RB ( sftp:// " and " scp:// )
sessions only.
.TP
.BI option " name value"
Configure session options:
.BR option\ confirm\ on|off
controls overwrite and delete confirmations.
.BR option\ batch\ on|off|abort|continue
controls batch mode. Scripts default to aborting after an error.
On and abort stop subsequent commands; off and continue proceed, but errors
still cause a nonzero process exit status.
.BR option\ resume\ on|off|automatic
controls automatic transfer resumption,
.BR option\ transfer\ binary|ascii|automatic
the mode of the following transfers, and
.BR option\ echo\ on|off
echoes each command before it runs.
.BR option\ editor\ program
and
.BR option\ editmaxsize\ bytes
choose the editor used by
.B edit
and the largest file it will open.
.BR option\ include\ mask " and " option\ exclude\ mask
set the persistent file mask (either one replaces both; the value
.B clear
resets it), with
.BR option\ failonnomatch\ on|off ,
.BR option\ synchdelete\ on|off
and
.BR option\ reconnecttime\ seconds|off
for the related behaviors.
.BR option\ preset\ name
makes a transfer preset the default of the following transfer commands
and
.B "option preset off"
stops applying presets altogether for this session.
A name or a value may be abbreviated when the abbreviation is unique;
masks, programs and numbers are never guessed. Called with no name,
.B option
prints every setting. An option this build does not implement fails by
name instead of pretending.
.TP
.B help
List the commands with a short description in two columns.
.BR "help" \ ICommand
prints the help of one command, aliases resolving to their command.
.TP
.B exit
End the session. The aliases
.BR bye ,
.B quit
and
.B end
also work.
.PP
Local and remote paths may contain the wildcards
.B *
and
.BR ? .
.PP
.B call
and
.B show
are rejected: the connection runs the SFTP subsystem only, so there is no
remote shell to run a command in.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
The keys held by a running ssh-agent are offered automatically when
.B SSH_AUTH_SOCK
is set, which is the simplest way to authenticate with an OpenSSH key.
.TP
.B LINSCP_PASSWORD
Password to use when
.B \-password=
is not given.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero if every command succeeded, non-zero otherwise.
.SH EXAMPLES
Upload everything in the current directory and list the result:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linscp \-batch \-command="open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \\
  \-hostkey=SHA256:abcdef... ; put * . ; ls ; exit"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a script:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cat > sync.txt <<'EOF'
open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \-hostkey=*
get *.html /var/www/html
exit
EOF
linscp \-batch \-script=sync.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Preview what a synchronization would do, then run it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linscp \-batch \-command="open sftp://user@example.com/var/www \-hostkey=* ; \\
  synchronize local \-criteria=time \-filemask=*.html \-preview ; \\
  synchronize local \-criteria=time \-filemask=*.html ; exit"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Keep two directories in step, deleting what the other side removed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linscp \-batch \-command="open webserver ; \\
  synchronize both \-delete /srv/site /var/www ; exit"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Store a site, then edit a file on it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linscp \-batch \-command="savesession webserver \-protocol=sftp \\
  \-host=example.com \-username=user \-remotedir=/var/www ; exit"
linscp \-batch \-command="open webserver ; edit index.html ; exit"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Start the graphical interface:
.PP
.RS
.nf
linscp \-gui
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
LinSCP does not implement full WinSCP feature parity, and Linux is the only
platform it is built, packaged and tested on. Native SFTP and native SCP
through the bundled PuTTY core, basic recursive transfers/resume,
synchronization, persistent sessions, a remote editor and a browser-based
graphical interface exist. The remaining protocols use curl and depend on its
build capabilities: when the installed curl lacks the protocol, opening such an
endpoint fails with that statement rather than reporting a session that cannot
transfer anything.
Remote WebDAV paths encode spaces and URL metacharacters in filenames rather
than treating them as a URL query or fragment. WebDAV provides PROPFIND
listings, MOVE and single-file COPY, and its locking and dead properties are
reachable through
.BR lock ,
.B unlock
and
.BR property ,
and a conditional request beyond a lock token is sent too:
.B etag
records an
.B If-Match
or
.B If-None-Match
tag per path, a WebDAV write of that path carries it in the same
.B If
header, and a write over a version this client has not read is refused with
412. S3 uses Signature
Version 4, supports object listing/stat/transfer and CopyObject, and uses
multipart uploads for files larger than 16 MiB; an interrupted multipart
upload restarts from the first part. ACLs, versioning, storage classes and
server-side encryption options are absent. SCP is WinSCP's remote-shell
implementation - listings, stat, directory and metadata commands over the
server's shell, transfers over its own
.B scp
- and the one thing it cannot do is continue a partial transfer, because the
rcp protocol has no offset to continue from; FTP uses a generic listing
parser. File masks, ASCII/automatic conversion, reconnect and transfer presets
are implemented; the .NET/PowerShell automation API and broad GUI parity are
not.
.PP
.B keepuptodate
reflects local changes on the remote side and runs in the foreground. A
recursive inotify watcher, plus a full comparison at each
.BI \-interval=
(10 seconds by default) as a safety rescan, drives it; a timer fallback is
used when the watch cannot be set up. A non-interactive run must use
.BR \-count= .
.PP
The graphical interface is a local web application, not a remote one: it is
reachable from the machine that runs it (or through a proxy you point at it
with
.IR \-allow\-host ),
and its API is protected by a per\-run token rather than by a user account,
so it expects to be the only thing talking to that port. The remote pane
queues file/folder drops from a file manager, including nested paths and
empty directories when the browser exposes file\-entry APIs. Local\-pane
drops and cross\-desktop compatibility are not established.
.PP
.B synchronize
both directions relies on a record of the previous synchronization written to
the state directory; without one, every file that exists on both sides is a
conflict. The graphical interface and the
.B \-server
protocol are intentionally undocumented interfaces for the browser client and
may change between releases.
See docs/upstream-parity.md for the pinned baseline and completion plan.
